# Cell-level gate: audit after the results

This page belongs to the cell-gate study in the [README](../README.md#cell-level-gate-confirmatory-study). Everything on it is exploratory: it was looked at after the results were known, and it changes no committed result, label or verdict. The counting rules are fixed in `writeup/inputs/audit_013_definition.txt`; the values come from `writeup/inputs/audit_013.json` (sha256 {{hash.audit_013}}), which reads the run records and the committed result file. Notation: p10 is the share of pairs where the candidate succeeds and S0 fails (an improvement), p01 the share where the candidate fails and S0 succeeds (a regression), and Δ = p10 − p01.

## What the Oracle effect estimate compares (Exploratory)

On the Oracle split, a candidate runs once (repeat zero, with the same LLM seed as S0's repeat zero), while S0 is the case-wise majority of its {{design.t009b.s0_oracle_repeats}} repeats. So the Oracle effect estimate of the cell-gate study is the mean, over the fixed Oracle incidents ({{design.t009b.oracle_per_cell}} per cell, equal cell weights), of a single candidate run minus the S0 majority. It is not the expected difference between two single-run policies. This was the registered design; the planning simulation of the null label rates used the same majority.

The table shows, from S0's own records, what this rule gives for a candidate that behaves exactly like S0: the cell mean of a single S0 run minus the majority, the same for S0's repeat zero, and the number of cases whose repeats disagree.

| Cell | Single run − majority | Repeat zero − majority | Cases with mixed repeats |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| bad_deployment/L1 | {{audit.oracle_asymmetry.cd.bad_deployment/L1.single_minus_majority}} | {{audit.oracle_asymmetry.cd.bad_deployment/L1.r0_minus_majority}} | {{audit.oracle_asymmetry.cd.bad_deployment/L1.mixed_cases}} of {{audit.oracle_asymmetry.cd.bad_deployment/L1.cases}} |
| bad_deployment/L2 | {{audit.oracle_asymmetry.cd.bad_deployment/L2.single_minus_majority}} | {{audit.oracle_asymmetry.cd.bad_deployment/L2.r0_minus_majority}} | {{audit.oracle_asymmetry.cd.bad_deployment/L2.mixed_cases}} of {{audit.oracle_asymmetry.cd.bad_deployment/L2.cases}} |
| dependency_failure/L1 | {{audit.oracle_asymmetry.cd.dependency_failure/L1.single_minus_majority}} | {{audit.oracle_asymmetry.cd.dependency_failure/L1.r0_minus_majority}} | {{audit.oracle_asymmetry.cd.dependency_failure/L1.mixed_cases}} of {{audit.oracle_asymmetry.cd.dependency_failure/L1.cases}} |
| dependency_failure/L2 | {{audit.oracle_asymmetry.cd.dependency_failure/L2.single_minus_majority}} | {{audit.oracle_asymmetry.cd.dependency_failure/L2.r0_minus_majority}} | {{audit.oracle_asymmetry.cd.dependency_failure/L2.mixed_cases}} of {{audit.oracle_asymmetry.cd.dependency_failure/L2.cases}} |
| traffic_spike/L1 | {{audit.oracle_asymmetry.cd.traffic_spike/L1.single_minus_majority}} | {{audit.oracle_asymmetry.cd.traffic_spike/L1.r0_minus_majority}} | {{audit.oracle_asymmetry.cd.traffic_spike/L1.mixed_cases}} of {{audit.oracle_asymmetry.cd.traffic_spike/L1.cases}} |
| traffic_spike/L2 | {{audit.oracle_asymmetry.cd.traffic_spike/L2.single_minus_majority}} | {{audit.oracle_asymmetry.cd.traffic_spike/L2.r0_minus_majority}} | {{audit.oracle_asymmetry.cd.traffic_spike/L2.mixed_cases}} of {{audit.oracle_asymmetry.cd.traffic_spike/L2.cases}} |

The table is on CD. On ES the offsets are zero except dependency_failure/L2 ({{audit.oracle_asymmetry.es.dependency_failure/L2.single_minus_majority}}), because S0's ES sits at the floor or the ceiling in every cell (see below).

**What this means for the labels.** The offsets reach the size of ε ({{design.epsilon}}) and of τ_cell ({{t009b.tau.cd.bad_deployment/L2}} in bad_deployment/L2).

- **Severe Local Harm.** The lowest cell of each of the {{audit.labels.severe.candidates}} Severe candidates lies between {{audit.labels.severe.worst_cell.closest_to_zero}} and {{audit.labels.severe.worst_cell.farthest}}, well beyond the largest offset. The Severe labels, and the RQ9-1 result built on them, stand as reported.
- **Local Harm without Severe.** Of the {{audit.labels.local_harm_only.candidates}} such candidates, {{audit.labels.local_harm_only.with_cell.bad_deployment/L2}} have bad_deployment/L2 below −τ, and {{audit.labels.local_harm_only.this_cell_only.bad_deployment/L2}} have no other cell below −τ. In that cell a candidate that behaves like S0 already starts near {{audit.oracle_asymmetry.cd.bad_deployment/L2.r0_minus_majority}}. These labels should be read as limited evidence of harm. No label is changed.

## Thresholds h and δ (Exploratory)

- δ(N) resamples cases without cell stratification and picks one random pair of S0 repeats per draw (`exact.null_diff_quantile_exact`, as registered); C4 uses a cell-stratified paired bootstrap (`gates.gate_c4`). The mismatch noted for the mean-gate study is still present here.
- h(n_c) is a quantile of the minimum over the six cells of S0-vs-S0 cell differences (`rq9.cell_threshold_h`), so it already acts jointly on the cells; on CD, h({{design.t009b.h_n}}) = {{t009b.h.cd.70.h}} with an achieved false rejection of {{t009b.h.cd.70.achieved_false_rejection}}. The uncertainty of h itself is not reported: its draws reuse the same repeat pairs and the same {{design.t009b.calibration_per_cell}} cases per cell.
- The calibration pairs use different LLM seeds, while every gate comparison pairs a candidate with S0 under the same seed. Different-seed S0 repeats disagreed on CD in {{audit.calibration_discordance.cd.all.differ}} of {{audit.calibration_discordance.cd.all.pairs}} pairs; by cell:

| Cell | CD disagreement between S0 calibration repeats |
| --- | --- |
| bad_deployment/L1 | {{audit.calibration_discordance.cd.bad_deployment/L1.differ}} of {{audit.calibration_discordance.cd.bad_deployment/L1.pairs}} |
| bad_deployment/L2 | {{audit.calibration_discordance.cd.bad_deployment/L2.differ}} of {{audit.calibration_discordance.cd.bad_deployment/L2.pairs}} |
| dependency_failure/L1 | {{audit.calibration_discordance.cd.dependency_failure/L1.differ}} of {{audit.calibration_discordance.cd.dependency_failure/L1.pairs}} |
| dependency_failure/L2 | {{audit.calibration_discordance.cd.dependency_failure/L2.differ}} of {{audit.calibration_discordance.cd.dependency_failure/L2.pairs}} |
| traffic_spike/L1 | {{audit.calibration_discordance.cd.traffic_spike/L1.differ}} of {{audit.calibration_discordance.cd.traffic_spike/L1.pairs}} |
| traffic_spike/L2 | {{audit.calibration_discordance.cd.traffic_spike/L2.differ}} of {{audit.calibration_discordance.cd.traffic_spike/L2.pairs}} |

The same-seed agreement of {{run.same_seed.same_disposition}} of {{run.same_seed.pairs}} pairs compares sequential with concurrent runs under two configurations, so it does not tell how much the same seed reduces disagreement in the gate comparisons.

