- **ES.** S0's ES is at the floor in {{audit.s0_rates.edges.es.floor}} of {{audit.s0_rates.edges.es.cells}} cells (every Oracle repeat and Dev-B) and at the ceiling in {{audit.s0_rates.edges.es.ceiling}}. In a floor cell p01 is zero, so a regression cannot appear on ES there; the ES cell condition could act only in the dependency cells. This is part of why C4 and C5 accepted the same candidates on ES; on Dev-B no candidate that C4 accepted on ES had a dependency cell below −h.
- **CD.** S0's CD is at the ceiling in {{audit.s0_rates.edges.cd.ceiling}} cells (Oracle majority) and at most a tenth in {{audit.s0_rates.edges.cd.near_floor}}. A ceiling cell can show only regressions (p10 is zero), a near-floor cell mostly improvements, so no cell measures both directions well.
- This structure is kept as it is: the cell-gate result is a result under this stress test, not under a balanced baseline.

## Shared LLM seeds across levels (Exploratory)

The LLM seed rule takes the family, the incident seed and the repeat, but not the level (`seeds.derive_llm_seed`). The L1 and L2 incidents that share an incident seed number are different incidents (the L2 generator uses its own random stream), but they get the same LLM seed.

| Split | Cases | Family and seed groups | Groups holding both an L1 and an L2 case |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Dev-B | {{audit.seed_sharing.t009_dev_b.cases}} | {{audit.seed_sharing.t009_dev_b.seed_groups}} | {{audit.seed_sharing.t009_dev_b.shared_by_l1_l2}} |
| Oracle | {{audit.seed_sharing.t009_oracle.cases}} | {{audit.seed_sharing.t009_oracle.seed_groups}} | {{audit.seed_sharing.t009_oracle.shared_by_l1_l2}} |
| Calibration | {{audit.seed_sharing.calibration.cases}} | {{audit.seed_sharing.calibration.seed_groups}} | {{audit.seed_sharing.calibration.shared_by_l1_l2}} |

So the L1 and L2 cells of one family are not independent at the seed level. How much this couples their outcomes is not measured.

## Candidate pairs on Dev-B (Exploratory)

All {{audit.dev_b_pairs.candidates}} proposer candidates have Dev-B records paired with S0's repeat zero: {{audit.dev_b_pairs.pairs}} pairs, {{audit.dev_b_pairs.same_seed}} of them with the same LLM seed. Each candidate and S0 ran once on Dev-B, so the candidate's own run-to-run variation cannot be estimated from these records.

## Idea groups (Exploratory)

The grouping method (same condition, same action or same added step; no gate or oracle values) is fixed in Section 6.6 of the cell-gate pre-registration, committed in {{commit.t009b_preregistration}} before any proposer call. The group file (sha256 {{hash.t009b_groups}}) was committed with the pool in {{commit.t009b_groups}}, before the run plan ({{commit.t009b_plan}}) and before the main run started ({{run.t009b.main.records.first_start}}). One rater (Claude) made the groups; there is no agreement check.

## What the Fisher and Clopper–Pearson results support (Exploratory)

- The Fisher p of RQ9-1 ({{t009b.rq.rq9_1.p}}) compares the set C5 rejected with a random rejection of the same size, inside this frozen pool and with the labels held fixed. It describes this pool.
- It is not evidence about a new population of candidates. The candidates share the S0 majority baseline, the LLM seeds and, within an idea group, the idea; the sensitivity check with one candidate per group ({{t009b.cluster_sensitivity.rq9_1.p}}) addresses only the last.
- The Clopper–Pearson interval of RQ9-3 treats the candidates as independent draws, which candidates of one idea group are not.

## Pairs in the Ticket 012 base-rate check (Exploratory)

The {{t012.stage0.attempts.0.disagreement.pairs}} same-seed pairs of the base-rate check pair the base run of each of {{audit.t012_pairs.cases}} Dev-A cases with each of two reruns, so every pair shares the base run with one other pair. The cases carry {{audit.t012_pairs.llm_seeds}} distinct LLM seeds (L1 and L2 share). Of the {{audit.t012_pairs.differing}} differing pairs, {{audit.t012_pairs.cases_both_pairs_differ}} cases contributed both of their pairs and {{audit.t012_pairs.cases_one_pair_differs}} cases one pair; the two reruns differed in {{audit.t012_pairs.reruns_differing}} cases, a comparison the rule did not use. The Clopper–Pearson bound behind k treats the pairs as independent, so its effective sample is smaller than the count. k was an exploratory rule and is not re-judged here.

